.TH TRESTLE.CONF 5 "October 2026" "trestle 0.1" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
trestle.conf \- configuration of the trestle container network plugin
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/trestle/trestle.conf
.br
.I /etc/trestle/conf.d/*.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
INI style file with one \fB[global]\fR section and one section per permitted
network, written \fB[network:\fINAME\fB]\fR (or \fB[network "\fINAME\fB"]\fR).
Lines starting with \fB#\fR or \fB;\fR are comments. Values may be quoted.
Boolean values accept yes/no, true/false, on/off, 1/0. Files in \fIconf.d\fR
ending in \fI.conf\fR are read after the main file in lexical order; a later
value replaces an earlier one, except \fBnft_rule\fR which accumulates.
Unknown keys are errors. Check a configuration with \fBtrestled \-t\fR.
.SH [global]
.TP
.B socket
Unix socket path. Default \fI/tmp/trestle.sock\fR.
.TP
.BR socket_owner ", " socket_group ", " socket_mode
Ownership and octal mode applied to the socket. Default root, root, 0777.
Restricting them (for example group \fIcontainers\fR, mode 0770) limits who
can talk to the daemon at all.
.TP
.B client_binary
Path of the trestle client. Connections from any other executable are
rejected. Default \fI/usr/libexec/trestle/trestle\fR.
.TP
.B server_binary
Path of trestled, checked by the client. Default \fI/usr/sbin/trestled\fR.
.TP
.B verify_server
\fBstrict\fR, \fBrelaxed\fR (default) or \fBno\fR. See
.BR trestle (8).
.TP
.B server_selinux_type
When set, the client requires the daemon's SELinux label to have this type
(for example \fBtrestled_t\fR).
.TP
.B state_dir
Default \fI/var/lib/trestle\fR.
.TP
.B hosts_path
Hosts file managed for networks with \fBhosts_file = yes\fR. Default
\fI/etc/hosts\fR.
.TP
.B log_level
error, warning, notice, info (default) or debug.
.TP
.B request_timeout
Seconds a client may take to send its request. Default 10.
.TP
.B ip_forward
Enable net.ipv4.ip_forward at start-up. Default no.
.TP
.BR nft_binary ", " nft_table
Default \fI/usr/sbin/nft\fR and \fBtrestle\fR (table \fBinet trestle\fR).
.TP
.B firewall_cleanup
Remove the nftables table when the daemon stops. Default yes.
.TP
.B networkmanager
\fBauto\fR (default), \fByes\fR or \fBno\fR. Before trestle creates a bridge
it checks whether NetworkManager is meant to manage an interface of that name
(a keyfile with \fBinterface-name=\fR or an ifcfg file with \fBDEVICE=\fR
names it). If not, and NetworkManager is running, it runs
\fBnmcli device set\fR \fIbridge\fR \fBmanaged no\fR right after creating the
bridge so NetworkManager leaves it alone (runtime setting, retried for a few
seconds until NetworkManager has seen the device). Bridges that already
exist are never changed. \fBauto\fR silently skips this when nmcli is
missing or NetworkManager is not running; \fByes\fR logs a warning then;
\fBno\fR never calls nmcli.
.TP
.B nmcli_binary
Default \fI/usr/bin/nmcli\fR.
.TP
.BR aardvark_binary ", " aardvark_dir ", " aardvark_port
aardvark-dns executable, its configuration directory and port. Defaults
\fI/usr/libexec/podman/aardvark-dns\fR, \fI/run/trestle/aardvark-dns\fR, 53.
.SH [network:NAME]
Only networks that have a section can be used, unless a \fB[network:*]\fR
section exists (see below). The name must match the CNI \fBname\fR or the
Podman network name.
.TP
.B users
Comma separated access list: user names, \fB@\fIgroup\fR, \fB*\fR (everybody).
Prefix an entry with \fB!\fR to deny; deny entries always win. Containers
started by root (e.g. Kubernetes) need \fBroot\fR or \fB*\fR. A network
without \fBusers\fR can not be used.
.TP
.B bridge
Use this bridge instead of \fBtbr\fR+md5. An existing bridge is used as is
and never deleted.
.TP
.B subnet
\fIbridge-address\fR/\fIprefix\fR, e.g. \fB10.0.0.1/24\fR: 10.0.0.1 is
assigned to the bridge, containers get 10.0.0.2\-10.0.0.254 and a default
route via 10.0.0.1. Without a subnet no addresses are configured. Prefix
8\-30.
.TP
.B mtu
MTU of the bridge and both veth ends.
.TP
.BR stp ", " vlan_filtering
Bridge options used when trestle creates the bridge. Default no.
.TP
.B uplink
Physical interface enslaved to the bridge.
.TP
.B hosts_file
Publish \fIuser\fR.\fIcontainer\fR.\fInetwork\fR.trestle in \fBhosts_path\fR
inside a managed block (atomic rewrite, mode/owner/SELinux label kept).
Default no.
.TP
.B dnsmasq_hosts
Write a hosts file for dnsmasq's \fBaddn-hosts\fR option. \fB%n\fR in the
path is replaced by the network name.
.TP
.B dnsmasq_pid_file
Send SIGHUP to this dnsmasq after updating the file.
.TP
.B aardvark_dns
For Netavark containers: run aardvark-dns on the bridge address and register
the container name, its aliases and the .trestle name. Containers get the
bridge address as DNS server and \fBdns.podman\fR as search domain.
.TP
.B podman_ipam
\fBnone\fR (default): trestle allocates addresses for all users of the
bridge, which avoids conflicts between users. \fBhost-local\fR: Podman
allocates and passes static addresses; conflicts are reported as errors.
.TP
.B firewall
Manage forward rules for this bridge in the trestle nftables table.
.TP
.B masquerade
Masquerade traffic from the subnet leaving through other interfaces.
.TP
.BR outbound_policy ", " inbound_policy
\fBaccept\fR or \fBdrop\fR for new connections from / to containers. Defaults
accept and drop. Established/related traffic is always accepted.
.TP
.B allow_inbound
\fBtcp:\fIport\fR[\fB\-\fIport\fR], \fBudp:\fIport\fR[\fB\-\fIport\fR] or
\fBicmp\fR, comma separated.
.TP
.B allow_inbound_from
Source addresses / CIDRs allowed to reach the containers.
.TP
.B allow_outbound_to
Destinations containers may reach when \fBoutbound_policy = drop\fR.
.TP
.B nft_rule
A raw nftables rule placed first in the network's inbound and outbound
chains. May be given several times.
.TP
.B create_script
Script run when the network is created: on \fBpodman network create\fR
(Netavark \fBcreate\fR) and whenever trestle creates the network's bridge
(for example on the first CNI ADD, or again after the bridge was removed
because the last container left).
.TP
.B setup_script
Script run after a container or pod has been attached (veth, address,
route, DNS entries in place).
.TP
.B teardown_script
Script run when a container or pod is removed from the network, before its
veth is deleted. A failing teardown script never stops the teardown.
.TP
.B script_timeout
Seconds a script may run before it and its process group are killed.
Default 30, maximum 600.
.TP
.B script_on_error
\fBignore\fR (default): a failing script is only logged. \fBfail\fR: a
failing create script makes the request fail, and a failing setup script
undoes the attachment and returns the error to the runtime.
.SH [network:*]
The catch-all section applies to every network name that has no
\fB[network:NAME]\fR section of its own; an explicit section always wins
and is not merged with the catch-all. All network keys may be used, with
these differences:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbridge\fR, \fBuplink\fR and \fBsubnet\fR are not allowed: every network
gets its own bridge \fBtbr\fR+md5(\fIname\fR), and a fixed subnet would
overlap.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet_pool\fR = \fInetwork\fR/\fIprefix\fR and \fBsubnet_prefix\fR =
\fIn\fR (default 24) give each network its own /\fIn\fR out of the pool,
the first free one not used by any other network. The bridge gets the first
address of it. The assignment is stored in
\fI/var/lib/trestle/wildcard/\fIname\fR and kept, also after the bridge has
been removed, so a network keeps its addresses. Remove that file (while no
container uses the network) to give the subnet back. Without
\fBsubnet_pool\fR the networks get no addresses, and features that need
them (\fBmasquerade\fR, \fBhosts_file\fR, \fBdnsmasq_hosts\fR,
\fBaardvark_dns\fR) are rejected.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdnsmasq_hosts\fR must contain \fB%n\fR so each network has its own file.
.PP
\fBusers\fR of the catch-all decides who may use, and thereby create, such
networks. \fBtrestled \-t\fR lists the networks created from it.
.PP
.nf
[network:*]
users = @containers
subnet_pool = 10.100.0.0/16
subnet_prefix = 24
masquerade = yes
firewall = yes
.fi
.SH HOOK SCRIPTS
Scripts must be absolute paths to executable regular files owned by root and
not writable by group or others, in a directory with the same restrictions;
otherwise they are refused. They run as root, in the trestled service
sandbox, with the event name as the first argument, standard input from
\fI/dev/null\fR and standard output and error copied line by line to the
trestled log. Requests on all networks wait while a script runs, so keep
scripts short. The environment contains only \fBPATH\fR, \fBLANG\fR and
the variables below; variables without a value are omitted.
.TP
.B TRESTLE_EVENT
create, setup or teardown (also the first argument).
.TP
.BR TRESTLE_MODE ", " TRESTLE_COMMAND ", " TRESTLE_VERSION
cni or netavark; the runtime command (ADD, DEL, create, setup, teardown); the
trestle version.
.TP
.BR TRESTLE_NETWORK ", " TRESTLE_BRIDGE ", " TRESTLE_BRIDGE_CREATED
Network name, bridge name, 1 if trestle created the bridge during this
request.
.TP
.BR TRESTLE_SUBNET ", " TRESTLE_PREFIX ", " TRESTLE_GATEWAY ", " TRESTLE_BRIDGE_IP ", " TRESTLE_BRIDGE_IP_CIDR
Addressing of the network (with \fBsubnet\fR).
.TP
.BR TRESTLE_MTU ", " TRESTLE_UPLINK
When configured.
.TP
.B TRESTLE_PODMAN_NETWORK_ID
Podman network id (create event from Netavark).
.TP
.BR TRESTLE_USER ", " TRESTLE_UID ", " TRESTLE_GID ", " TRESTLE_ROOTLESS
The user running the container (from the socket credentials) and 1 when the
client ran rootless.
.TP
.BR TRESTLE_CONTAINER_ID ", " TRESTLE_CONTAINER_NAME ", " TRESTLE_CONTAINER_ALIASES
Container id, name (CNI: \fInamespace\fR-\fIpod\fR) and comma separated
Netavark DNS aliases.
.TP
.BR TRESTLE_K8S_POD_NAME ", " TRESTLE_K8S_POD_NAMESPACE
From \fBCNI_ARGS\fR.
.TP
.B TRESTLE_FQDN
\fIuser\fR.\fIcontainer\fR.\fInetwork\fR.trestle
.TP
.BR TRESTLE_HOST_IFNAME ", " TRESTLE_CONTAINER_IFNAME
Host side (\fBtrv\fR...) and container side interface names.
.TP
.BR TRESTLE_HOST_MAC ", " TRESTLE_CONTAINER_MAC
MAC addresses of the two ends.
.TP
.BR TRESTLE_IP ", " TRESTLE_IP_CIDR
The container address, when the network has a subnet.
.TP
.B TRESTLE_NETNS
Network namespace path as given by the runtime. For rootless containers it
is only valid in the caller's mount namespace.
.TP
.BR TRESTLE_NETNS_FD ", " TRESTLE_NETNS_FD_PATH
setup only: the container network namespace is open as file descriptor 3,
usable for example with \fBnsenter \-\-net=/proc/self/fd/3\fR. This works for
rootless containers too.
.PP
The container and interface variables are absent for the create event.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
[global]
socket_group = containers
socket_mode = 0770
ip_forward = yes

[network:lab]
users = @developers, !intern
subnet = 10.42.0.1/24
mtu = 1450
hosts_file = yes
aardvark_dns = yes
firewall = yes
masquerade = yes
allow_inbound = tcp:22, tcp:8080, icmp

[network:lan]
users = *
setup_script = /etc/trestle/hooks/lan.sh
teardown_script = /etc/trestle/hooks/lan.sh
bridge = br0
uplink = eno2
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trestle (8),
.BR trestled (8),
.BR nft (8)
